.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1225 AM PDT Thu Aug 13 2026
(Today and tonight)

The upper-level low remains off Point Conception and will continue
to bring up to a 15% chance for elevated rain showers/thunderstorms
through early Thursday morning across the Central Coast as it moves
inland. This will bring near-critical fire weather conditions
through Thursday early morning across Monterey and San Benito
Counties. Erratic/gusty outflow winds up to 40 mph can be expected
with any elevated rain showers/thunderstorms. The main concern is
lightning which will pose the risk for new fire starts and has the
potential to hinder operations on the Timber Fire. Smoke from the
Timber Fire will continue to be transported into the Central Coast
via light southwesterly winds. Smoke will get trapped under the
inversion at night/in the morning, making the appearance and smell
worse. The proximity to the low will allow for a relatively deeper
marine layer across the Central Coast (2,000 feet) than the Bay Area
(1,800 feet). Sensible weather wise, the cooling and moistening
trend will continue through Friday with temperatures well below
normal.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1225 AM PDT Thu Aug 13 2026
(Friday through Wednesday)

Friday will see the coolest conditions of the week with well below
normal temperatures and a deep marine layer as upper-level troughing
persists across the West Coast. By the weekend, upper-level ridging
will build in to the Desert Southwest and an upper-level low will
develop well off the West Coast. This will lead to cooler conditions
with a deeper marine layer in the Bay Area and hotter conditions
with a more compressed marine layer for the Central Coast.
Temperatures will remain below normal with the higher terrain
moderating to near normal with the help of warming 850 mb
temperatures.

